.TH MACHO\-A2O 1 "October 3, 2026" "MachO-Tools 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
macho\-a2o \- map a virtual address to a file offset
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B macho\-a2o
.I hex-vaddr
.RI [ hex-vaddr ...]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-arch= name ]
.RB [ \-b | \-\-binary
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reports which byte of the file each given virtual address corresponds to, together
with the section it falls in and the slice it belongs to.
.PP
Every address a tool reports is a virtual address, because that is what a call site
or a symbol refers to. Every position a hex editor, a
.BR dd (1)
pipeline or a patch script wants is a file offset. Converting between them means
reading the section table by hand.
.PP
Both offsets are reported.
.B offset
is slice\-relative, matching what the section table records, and
.B absoluteOffset
adds the slice's own position in the file. On a thin binary they are equal. On a
universal binary they are not, and reporting only one of them is how a reader ends
up editing the wrong bytes: in
.IR /bin/ls ,
whose x86_64 slice begins at 0x4000,
.B __TEXT
at relative offset 0 is at absolute 0x4000.
.SH ZERO\-FILL
.BR __bss ,
.B __noptrbss
and
.B __PAGEZERO
have addresses and sizes but no bytes in the file: the loader supplies zeros, and
the linker records such a section's file offset as 0. An address there is
.IR mapped ,
so reporting "not found" would be wrong, and reporting a file offset would be
worse, since there is no byte to read.
.PP
Such an address is reported as
.B zerofill
with no offset, which is a third answer distinct from both "mapped to a byte" and
"not in this binary". It counts as a successful run.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-arch= name
Read one architecture,
.B x86_64
or
.BR arm64 .
A universal binary maps
.B __TEXT
at 0x100000000 in
.I every
slice, so without this flag an address in it is reported as ambiguous rather than
guessed.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-binary " " \fIpath\fR
The binary to read. Required in practice: every positional argument is an address,
so a path cannot be told apart from a query by position.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON object to standard output and send all prose to standard error.
Addresses are rendered as
.B "0x..."
strings: a 64\-bit virtual address does not survive a JSON number.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MACHO_BINARY
The binary to read when none is given.
.TP
.B MACHO_APP
An application bundle to read, tried after
.BR MACHO_BINARY .
.TP
.B MACHO_CONFIG
Path to a replacement
.IR config.json .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one address was mapped, whether or not it reached a byte.
.TP
.B 1
The tool ran and no address was mapped by any slice. A negative result, not a
failure.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, including an address that is not hexadecimal with an
.B 0x
prefix.
.TP
.B 3
The file could not be read or parsed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-a2o 0x100001000 \-b /usr/local/go/bin/go
macho\-a2o \-\-json 0x100001000 \-b /usr/local/go/bin/go | jq '.data.queries[0].offset'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read six bytes from the file at an address reported by another tool:
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-a2o \-\-json 0x100085c30 \-b /usr/local/go/bin/go |
  jq -r '.data.queries[0].absoluteOffset' |
  xargs -I{} dd if=/usr/local/go/bin/go bs=1 skip={} count=6 2>/dev/null
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
Every positional argument is an address, so the binary comes from
.B \-b
rather than from the last positional. This is the same rule
.BR macho\-symlookup (1)
follows, and for the same reason: a path guessed from whether it looks like a
query turns a typo into a confident wrong answer.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR macho\-o2a (1),
.BR macho\-symlookup (1),
.BR macho\-describe (1),
.BR otool (1),
.BR nm (1)